{"answer":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9:\n\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. Exhaustiveness: the empty set sums to 0; no singleton can sum to 9 because the largest available element is 8; and four distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus only two- and three-element subsets need be considered. The exhaustive increasing-tuple enumerations in the proof steps give four pairs and three triples, hence 7 total.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition candidate subsets by cardinality, eliminate cardinalities 0, 1, and at least 4, then enumerate increasing pairs and triples.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This is a self-contained exhaustive argument. Both supplied candidate approaches agree on this route; the enumeration below supplies the supporting argument."},{"description":"Enumerate unrestricted partitions of 9 and then filter to distinct parts at most 8.","id":"partition-filter","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It introduces an unnecessary separate enumeration and is less direct than cardinality casework."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9\".","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-problem","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The ground set is {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}, and the target element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the exhaustive cardinality case split and enumerations in the listed proof steps.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-problem"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have element-sum 9.","step_ids":["exclude-small","exclude-large","enumerate-pairs","enumerate-triples","count-solutions"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The lists are derived exhaustively in enumerate-pairs and enumerate-triples.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-problem"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"solution-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The solution subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["enumerate-pairs","enumerate-triples"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct arithmetic verification in verify-example.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["given-problem"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-solution","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a subset of the ground set with element-sum 9.","step_ids":["verify-example"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"exclude-small","justification":"The empty set has sum 0. A singleton has sum equal to its sole member, and every member of the ground set is at most 8.","statement":"No solution has cardinality 0 or 1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"exclude-large","justification":"A subset of at least four distinct positive integers has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10; adding further positive elements cannot decrease this sum.","statement":"No solution has cardinality at least 4."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"enumerate-pairs","justification":"Write a two-element subset as {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9. Then a<9/2, so a is 1, 2, 3, or 4. The respective values b=8, 7, 6, and 5 are in the ground set. These exhaust all possibilities for a.","statement":"The two-element solutions are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"enumerate-triples","justification":"Write a three-element subset as {a,b,c} with a<b<c and a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b<c, giving (b,c)=(2,6) or (3,5). If a=2, then b+c=7 with 2<b<c, giving (b,c)=(3,4). If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12, impossible.","statement":"The three-element solutions are exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["exclude-small","exclude-large","enumerate-pairs","enumerate-triples"],"id":"count-solutions","justification":"Every subset has one cardinality. The exclusion steps leave only the disjoint cardinalities 2 and 3, whose exhaustive lists contain 4 and 3 subsets respectively. Therefore the total is 4+3=7.","statement":"The listed solutions are exhaustive and their number is 7."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"verify-example","justification":"Both 1 and 8 belong to the ground set, and 1+8=9.","statement":"{1,8} is a solution."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
